---
// Reference / CLI / doctor.
//
// Written rather than generated: the binary's own surface, not a catalog
// command. See init.astro for why these live under src/pages.
//
// The field list is deliberately described rather than pasted in full: the
// inference block reports what this machine has, so a paste would be a claim
// about the reader's laptop.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CliBlock from '../../../components/docs/CliBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  editPath="src/pages/docs/reference/doctor.astro"
  title="doctor"
  description="Check the installation, and the health of a database when you name one. The first thing to run when something is not behaving."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>doctor</h1>
  <p>
    <code>strata doctor</code> reports what this machine has: which binary is on the path, where the home
    directory resolved to, whether inference can run, and anything it considers an issue.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata doctor</CliBlock>
  <CliBlock variant="output"
    >binary 1.2.4 database - home /home/you/.strata inference local_execution false
    models_catalogued 11 models_dir /home/you/.strata/models models_downloaded 0 ready_providers
    openai issues - path_ok true platform linux-x86_64 user_config path
    /home/you/.config/strata/config.toml state readable</CliBlock
  >

  <h2>Reading it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>database</code> is <code>-</code> because no database was named. Name one and it reports that
    database's health alongside the installation:
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata ./my-db doctor</CliBlock>
  <CliBlock variant="output"
    >database exists true info branch_count 1 created false default_branch default durable true
    memory_budget source derived_from_host total_bytes 6846744576 usable_host_bytes 27386978304 open
    true space_count 1 target durable_local version 1.2.4 open_ok true path /home/you/my-db</CliBlock
  >
  <p>
    <code>open_ok</code> is the one to read when a database will not open: it separates "the path is not
    there" from "the path is there and something is wrong with it". <code>version</code> is the engine
    that wrote the database, which is not necessarily the binary you are holding.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>path_ok</code> answers the question behind most installation problems: is the binary you just
    ran the one on your <code>PATH</code>. A false here usually means a second copy earlier in the
    path.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>issues</code> is the field to read first. When it is <code>-</code> there is nothing to report;
    anything listed there is what to fix.
  </p>

  <h2>The inference block</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part that answers "why can I not generate anything". <code>local_execution</code> says
    whether this build can run a model on the machine at all, which depends on how the binary was built,
    not on what is installed. <code>models_downloaded</code> is how many local models are actually present,
    as distinct from <code>models_catalogued</code>, which is how many the build knows how to fetch.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>ready_providers</code> lists cloud providers considered usable. Read it as <em
      >a key is present</em
    >, not as <em>a key works</em>: a provider appears here on the strength of configuration alone,
    and a key that is expired or wrong still shows as ready. Tracked as strata-core issue 3408.
  </p>

  <h2>Configuration</h2>
  <p>
    <code>user_config</code> reports the file's path and its state, so a configuration that is not being
    picked up can be diagnosed without guessing where the file should have been. <code>state</code> distinguishes
    a file that is readable from one that is missing or unreadable.
  </p>

  <h2>Scripting it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--json</code> returns the same facts as one document, which is the form to check in a setup
    script or a container build. The fields are the same; nesting matches the blocks above.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata --json doctor</CliBlock>
  <p>
    <code>doctor</code> reports. It does not repair anything, and it does not change the installation,
    so it is safe to run anywhere, including against a database another process is hosting.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
